123	<header>
124	  <div>
125	    <div class="eyebrow">Temporal AI Challenge · error404 · DB trip delay</div>
126	    <h1>Trip Delay Run Ledger</h1>
127	    <p class="lede">What we are building, every model we trained tonight, how well each one did, and what is still running. Times are Zurich; the hackathon ends Saturday 12:00.</p>
128	  </div>
129	  <div class="status">As of<b>Sat 13 Sep, 02:35</b></div>
130	</header>
131	
132	<section>
133	  <h2>What we are building</h2>
134	  <p>A traveller shares a trip: the train they are on and the stop they want to reach. We answer two questions at that moment: <strong>how late will the train arrive there</strong>, and <strong>will it be cancelled</strong>. The answer is not a single number but a range with probabilities, which we turn into plain sentences: "about +1 min, likely 0 to 5, 92% chance you are on time" and, for a connection, "96% chance of making your change".</p>
135	  <p>The model is a <strong>time-series language model</strong>: Llama 3.2 (3 billion parameters) from the OpenTSLM project, lightly fine-tuned by us. It gets the live situation as input (how late the train was at each stop so far, how this route usually does, how the station and the whole network are doing right now) and writes an answer in minutes. Because every minute from 0 to 999 is a single word to this model, we can read off the probability of every possible delay in one go, which is what gives us the range.</p>
136	  <div class="kpis">
137	    <div class="kpi"><b>298,430</b><span>training and test examples, each one a real train at a real moment with a real target stop</span></div>
138	    <div class="kpi"><b>Nov 25 to Aug 26</b><span>public DB timetable data. Trained on data up to 1 May, tuned on May, tested on June to August</span></div>
139	    <div class="kpi"><b>21,982</b><span>extra September examples, scraped after the training data was frozen: a true "future" test</span></div>
140	    <div class="kpi"><b>2 answers</b><span>delay range in minutes, plus the chance of cancellation</span></div>
141	  </div>
142	</section>
143	
144	<section>
145	  <h2>The pieces <span class="eyebrow">everything that exists besides the model</span></h2>
146	  <div class="pipe">
147	    <article><h3>Data preparation</h3><p>Turns raw monthly timetable files into examples. Every input is something that was known <em>before</em> the prediction moment, so the model can never peek at the answer.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill good">done</span></div></article>
148	    <article><h3>Yardstick models</h3><p>Three simpler models to compare against. The important one is <strong>XGBoost</strong>, a classic and very strong machine-learning method (gradient-boosted decision trees) that gets exactly the same information as our model. If we cannot beat it, the language model brings nothing.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill good">done</span></div></article>
149	    <article><h3>Scoring</h3><p>Every model is scored on the same test trips with the same script: how far off the "most likely" delay was, how good the range was, and how well the cancellation chance was calibrated. Split out by train type and by how far ahead the prediction was made.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill good">done</span></div></article>
150	    <article><h3>Luck check</h3><p>A statistical test that tells whether a difference between two models is real or could be a fluke of which trips landed in the test set. Explained under the chart below.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill good">done tonight</span></div></article>
151	    <article><h3>Blending</h3><p>Instead of picking one model, mix the language model's forecast with XGBoost's. Tonight we learned that the best mix depends on how close the train is to the stop.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill good">done tonight</span></div></article>
152	    <article><h3>TimeNet connector and dataset</h3><p>The challenge asks for data in TimeNet's format. Our connector downloads, builds and converts the data; the finished dataset (298,430 records) passed TimeNet's own check. A speed bug in the conversion was found and fixed tonight.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill good">done tonight</span></div></article>
153	    <article><h3>Demo</h3><p>A command-line tool takes a trained model and real trips and prints the traveller view, including the chance of making a connection. Tested on two ICE trains meeting at Göttingen this morning. There is no web interface yet.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill warn">CLI only</span></div></article>
154	    <article><h3>Compute</h3><p>One GPU box of our own for the long run (about 1 second per training step) and rented H100 GPUs on Nebius for everything else (0.3 seconds per step). Six H100 jobs tonight.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill accent">running</span></div></article>
155	    <article><h3>Documentation</h3><p>DESIGN.md holds every decision (sections 1 to 15), tonight's results (16) and how they relate to published research (17). All committed and pushed.</p><div class="state"><span class="pill good">current</span></div></article>
156	  </div>
157	</section>
