344	<section>
345	  <h2>What we know for sure <span class="eyebrow">each of these passed the luck check</span></h2>
346	  <div class="findings">
347	    <div class="finding"><div><h3>Mixing our model with XGBoost by lead time beats XGBoost on all test trips</h3><p>Checked on 88,479 trips that actually ran: the mix is better by 0.028 minutes of CRPS (the plausible range is 0.019 to 0.039, all on the winning side) and by 0.035 minutes of median error. The mixing ratio was chosen on May data only and then applied blind to June to August.</p></div><div class="stat"><b>−0.028</b><span>CRPS vs XGBoost, full test</span></div></div>
348	    <div class="finding"><div><h3>On its own, our model wins the last quarter hour before arrival</h3><p>For predictions made within 15 minutes of the planned arrival (36,556 trips), its best guess is off by 0.991 minutes on average against XGBoost's 1.072, and its range is better by 0.055 CRPS. Further out, XGBoost is better (by 0.076 CRPS beyond 30 minutes). That is exactly why the mix uses our model close to arrival and XGBoost further away.</p></div><div class="stat"><b>0.99 vs 1.07</b><span>minutes off, last 15 min</span></div></div>
349	    <div class="finding neutral"><div><h3>The special time-series encoder is not pulling its weight</h3><p>Writing the numbers into the prompt as plain text beats feeding them through OpenTSLM's time-series encoder, with the same training otherwise (0.057 CRPS better, range 0.026 to 0.090). Whether the encoder starts pretrained or from scratch makes no difference. Published research says the same: language models read short number sequences well as text, and the encoder mainly pays off for very long signals such as ECGs.</p></div><div class="stat"><b>−0.057</b><span>text vs encoder, CRPS</span></div></div>
350	    <div class="finding neutral"><div><h3>The simpler training signal works better</h3><p>Rewarding only the exact right minute ("plain loss") gives better ranges than our original signal that also rewarded near misses: 0.041 CRPS better (range 0.011 to 0.069). Training longer keeps helping. Doubling the learning rate changes nothing.</p></div><div class="stat"><b>−0.041</b><span>plain vs original, CRPS</span></div></div>
351	    <div class="finding"><div><h3>Text input plus the plain loss beats XGBoost on its own</h3><p>The first single model to pass the luck check on the 5,000 trips: 0.064 CRPS better (range 0.005 to 0.137) and 0.063 minutes better on median error, after only 2,000 training steps. Mixed with XGBoost by lead time it reaches 3.618 / 2.901, the best numbers on this page (CRPS 0.070 better, range 0.030 to 0.127).</p></div><div class="stat"><b>−0.064</b><span>alone vs XGBoost, CRPS</span></div></div>
352	    <div class="finding neutral"><div><h3>Teaching the encoder did not work, and more history did not help</h3><p>Keeping the raw scale, mixing in series-reading questions, thirty days of hourly data through the encoder, and multi-scale history summaries in the text: none of them beat the plain 72-hour setup on the test trips (all within 0.01 of it or worse). An identical repeat of the best recipe differs by 0.006 CRPS, so these are real "no effect" results, not noise. The model already extracts what these inputs carry; the remaining error sits elsewhere (mostly in long lead times and rare large delays).</p></div><div class="stat"><b>0.006</b><span>noise between identical runs</span></div></div>
353	    <div class="finding"><div><h3>The story survives a true out-of-time test</h3><p>On 10,000 September trips, scraped after the training data was frozen, the shape is the same as on June to August: within 15 minutes of arrival our text model beats XGBoost (CRPS −0.011, range −0.021 to −0.002), beyond 30 minutes it loses, and the lead-time blend with ratios fitted on May and applied blind wins overall (CRPS −0.016, range −0.027 to −0.006; median error −0.019). Alone overall it is behind (+0.040). The long runs get the same test.</p></div><div class="stat"><b>−0.016</b><span>blend vs XGBoost, September</span></div></div>
354	    <div class="finding warn"><div><h3>The probabilities are already well calibrated</h3><p>We tried two standard ways of re-scaling the model's confidence after training; the best setting was "leave it as is". The cancellation chance in the mix comes mostly from XGBoost, which is better at it. Every free-text answer the model wrote could be parsed into a number.</p></div><div class="stat"><b>no change</b><span>calibration</span></div></div>
355	  </div>
