372	  <h2>Submission checklist <span class="eyebrow">what the challenge asks for</span></h2>
373	  <div class="check">
374	    <div><span class="pill warn">gap</span><div>Working demo<span>The command-line demo works on real September trips; there is no web page yet, and nobody owns the frontend folder.</span></div></div>
375	    <div><span class="pill good">ok</span><div>Code and training configuration<span>All in the repository; a one-page list of every run with its exact command is still to write.</span></div></div>
376	    <div><span class="pill warn">nearly</span><div>Trained model (checkpoint)<span>Every run's model (107 MB) is in cloud storage; the final one needs a fixed download address.</span></div></div>
377	    <div><span class="pill good">ok</span><div>Dataset documentation<span>DESIGN.md, the TimeNet dataset card, and the verified TimeNet dataset in cloud storage.</span></div></div>
378	    <div><span class="pill good">ok</span><div>Evaluation against baselines<span>Time-based split, a true future test (September), the luck check on every claim, and an honest negative result about the encoder.</span></div></div>
379	    <div><span class="pill good">bonus</span><div>Reusable TimeNet connector<span>Built and verified.</span></div></div>
380	    <div><span class="pill">skipped</span><div>Agentic dataset pipeline<span>Second bonus item; not started.</span></div></div>
381	  </div>
382	</section>
383	
384	<section>
385	  <h2>Glossary</h2>
386	  <dl class="glossary">
387	    <dt>XGBoost</dt><dd>A widely used, very strong machine-learning method based on decision trees. It is our yardstick: it gets the same inputs as our model, so any gain over it is due to the language model, not to better data.</dd>
388	    <dt>Median error</dt><dd>Take the model's single most likely delay, compare it with what really happened, average the difference in minutes over all trips that ran. Lower is better.</dd>
389	    <dt>CRPS</dt><dd>Continuous Ranked Probability Score, the standard score for probabilistic forecasts since the 1970s (weather services grade rain and temperature forecasts with it; forecasting competitions use it for ranges). It scores the whole predicted range rather than a single guess: low when the range is narrow and centred on what happened, high when it is wide or off. It is in minutes, and for a forecast with no spread it equals the plain error, so it can be compared with the median error directly. Lower is better. Reference: Gneiting and Raftery, Journal of the American Statistical Association, 2007.</dd>
390	    <dt>Lead time</dt><dd>How many minutes before the planned arrival the prediction was made. Predicting 5 minutes ahead is easy; 2 hours ahead is hard. Our buckets: up to 15, 15 to 30, 30 to 60, 60 to 120, more than 120 minutes.</dd>
391	    <dt>Luck check (paired bootstrap)</dt><dd>Randomly re-draw the test trips 2,000 times, score both models on each re-draw, and look at the range of differences. If the range does not include zero, one model wins consistently and the difference is not a fluke of the sample.</dd>
392	    <dt>Blend</dt><dd>An average of two models' forecasts. "80% ours, 20% XGBoost" means the forecast range is four parts ours and one part XGBoost. The lead-time blend uses a different ratio depending on how far the train is from the stop; the ratios were chosen on May data and then frozen.</dd>
393	    <dt>Plain loss vs original loss</dt><dd>The training signal. The original also gave partial credit for near misses (predicting 3 when the truth was 4) and for a well-shaped range; the plain version only rewards the exact minute. Simpler turned out better.</dd>
394	    <dt>Text only vs encoder</dt><dd>Two ways of giving the model its input series. "Encoder" is OpenTSLM's dedicated component that converts a number series into something the language model can read; "text only" simply writes the numbers into the prompt.</dd>
395	    <dt>Steps</dt><dd>Training iterations. Each step shows the model a batch of 8 examples; 12,000 steps is roughly half a pass over the training data.</dd>
396	  </dl>
397	</section>
398	
399	<footer>Numbers come from DESIGN.md section 16 and the scoring reports on our GPU box; every row on the 5,000 shared trips is scored by the same script. This page is a snapshot; it is updated as runs finish.</footer>
400	</div>
